From 2c93f111e5da6348f8501e96b292eddd0e837cd4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DanConwayDev <DanConwayDev@protonmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 19:27:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] test(push): bound and clean up the fallback proxy

The Darwin package review spent six hours in the fallback regression.
Its proxy handled clients sequentially and joined an input relay after
closing only the write half of the socket. An unfinished first session
could prevent system Git from receiving its advertisement.

Serve clients concurrently, close both socket halves during teardown,
join fixture workers, and reap receive-pack children on error paths.
Run the synchronous fallback test in an isolated child with a 60-second
deadline and a captured log tail. On Unix its dedicated process group
also bounds descendant Git processes. Production push behavior and the
assertion that two connections perform the fallback remain unchanged.

Add regressions for a second client while the first session is open and
for termination of a stalled isolated test. All three focused tests pass
on Linux when compiled against the cached ngit library and dependencies;
rustfmt and whitespace checks pass. Full package and Darwin validation
remain pending; the precise Darwin blocking point is not yet confirmed.

Assisted-by: Codex (GPT-6)
